{"kind":"assert","text":"Recording hugh's direction on README positioning and performance wording, so the implementing head has a durable basis rather than resting on a conversation.\n\nHIS RULINGS, in his words as nearly as I can put them. First, MCP and human-agent collaboration should be a primary part of the introduction and positioning, not a late mention. Second, on the performance figure: the roughly ten writes per second in the README came from the first measurement and may depend widely on context, so if the README makes a claim at all it should be quite vague, the word he used was few, and it should link to the saved performance numbers in the repository itself.\n\nWHAT I FOUND THAT SHARPENS THE SECOND RULING. There are two uncited numbers in that paragraph, not one. README line 114 carries roughly ten writes per second, and the next sentence carries raw read performance in the region of one hundred thousand events per second. Hugh spoke about the first. The second has the same defect and I intend to treat it the same way, because leaving one uncited figure beside a newly-hedged one would look like we hedged only the unflattering number. I am naming that here rather than doing it silently, and if he wants the read figure kept as it is I will put it back.\n\nTHE DISCIPLINE ALREADY EXISTS AND THE README BREAKS IT. docs/reference/performance.md states that performance measurements are evidence from one named workload and machine, not limits or service-level guarantees. That is exactly right and it is the standard the README fails: neither README number names a workload or a machine, and the ten cannot be reconciled by a reader with the retained evidence, which records a one-base append p50 in the region of four hundred and thirty milliseconds at a depth-one-thousand fixture on a named machine. A reader cannot tell whether these describe the same thing.\n\nAND THE HEADROOM IS ON FILE, which is why a frozen number would be wrong rather than merely vague. The append-fixed lane already removed roughly sixteen milliseconds of fixed cost, and a fifty millisecond absolute-append budget sits in a parked request. So current cost is implementation rather than design, it is known to be improvable, and identified optimizations have not yet been taken. A README figure would be falsified by work we have already authorized.\n\nWHAT I WILL WRITE, so it is on the record before I write it. A new opening that leads with agent-accountable state and names MCP as the primary interface, keeping the existing blog and video links. A performance paragraph that says signed writes cost more than an unsigned log, that most of that cost is implementation rather than design and that identified optimizations remain untaken, that the rate is a few writes per second on an ordinary machine, and that points at docs/reference/performance.md for measured results with their commits and environments. No standalone figure that a later optimization would falsify.\n\nONE THING I AM DELIBERATELY NOT DOING. The external reviewer's competitive claims about Temporal, Restate, DBOS and ordinary MCP servers will not appear. Two of the three are falsifiable quickly: agent frameworks do have persistence, and MCP servers can and do keep logs. The defensible contrast is a property one, between systems that persist state so a program can resume and a record anyone can verify, and even that belongs in the blog rather than the README, which names no competitor today. A positioning page that misdescribes another system costs us more than a vaguer one.\n\nI will implement this as self-initiated work on a branch, publish an artifact at the README path, and request independent review before anything merges. Hugh reviews the substance; I am not treating his direction here as approval of whatever prose I produce."}